    flattening trap.
  - PaperList channel 2 (page_001.png): the Paper ID column is colour-banded. 1-1 through 1-11
    are one contiguous green band; 2-1 starts a new blue band. This independently confirms the
    merged cell spans exactly 1-1..1-11.
  - PaperList page_005.txt lists the five topic labels in order: OS History and Architecture,
    Scheduling, Memory Management, Storage and File Systems, Threads/Events/Concurrency/Sync.
    Matches the ID-prefix grouping 1-,2-,3-,5-,4-.
  CONCLUSION (cross-checked, 3 independent signals): the 'OS History and Architecture' group is
  papers 1-1 .. 1-11, eleven papers. This is what Ex 1.9 must draw from.
  Caveat recorded: the spreadsheet-to-PDF text extraction is column-major, so page_003.txt
  gives titles in a different row order than the CSV (Machine-Independent VM lands last).
  I trusted the CSV for the ID<->title mapping and the PNG for the grouping.

Cut (on independence, not numbering):
  p1 = Ex 1.1-1.4   plain short-answer, 20 pts
  p2 = Ex 1.5-1.8   plain short-answer, 20 pts
  p3 = Ex 1.9       30 pts, three papers FROM the list
  p4 = Ex 1.0       30 pts, three papers found by SEARCH

Rationale:
  - 1.1-1.8 are eight mutually independent textbook questions; specs.md says "just a few
    sentences sufficient". Eight in one worker dilutes attention; eight workers is wasteful
    (each answer is ~4 sentences). Split 4+4. They share nothing, so any cut is safe.
  - 1.9 and 1.0 are deliberately kept as two separate workers BUT sequenced: p3 runs first and
    fixes its three papers, then p4's brief names those three explicitly as forbidden, plus the
    whole 1-1..1-11 list. Running them in parallel or briefing them identically is the obvious
    way this assignment produces two overlapping answers, which would lose marks on 1.0.
  - Ex "1.0" is almost certainly a typo for Ex 1.10 (it is last, worth 30 pts). Transcribed
    verbatim as "Ex. 1.0" and rendered that way; not silently renumbered.

Transcription note for p1: Ex 1.4 in the source reads \segmentation fault (core dumped)."
