## Master round 2 -> PASS, one new minor defect (D2.1), self-inflicted. Fixing and resubmitting.

The validator ran a check it had skipped in round 1: a >=10-word verbatim-overlap scan of the
paper fragments against the six source PDFs, against the assignment's explicit "you cannot
just copy the answers, put the answers in your own way". It found two unquoted verbatim runs
in p4's Mach summary -- and one of them, 16 words, was introduced BY my own D1.5(b) fix in
round 2. I had bought factual precision by adopting the paper's phrasing. That is a real
regression and it is against an explicit instruction in the assignment's front matter, so it
is worth the round to fix even though the validator passed the document.

Rather than trust the validator's list, I reimplemented the scan myself (normalise LaTeX away,
lowercase, strip punctuation, sliding 10-grams against pdftotext of all six PDFs) and ran it
over both fragments. It reproduced the two runs it named, and also surfaced two more in p3
that its own summary had waved through as "unavoidable technical phrasing". I fixed all four:

 p4 (i)   "independent page size is a boot time variable that is a power of two of the"
          -> "is fixed at boot as a power-of-two multiple of the machine-dependent one".
          Note this keeps the D1.5(b) correction intact: "power-of-two multiple" still says
          exactly what the paper says, in different words. Precision was not traded back.
 p4 (ii)  "address space and a set of port rights; a thread is the ..." -> reworded.
 p3 (iii) "an unbounded number of tasks for the segment controller and the system" -> reworded.
 p3 (iv)  "the processor is explicitly revoked at the end of a time slice" -> reworded.

Re-ran the scan afterwards. Every remaining >=10-word window in both fragments now traces to
either (a) a passage the answer explicitly puts inside quotation marks and attributes -- six
of these, which is legitimate quotation and not what the instruction forbids -- or (b) a
citation string in the reference list (venue names, author names, paper titles), which cannot
be reworded without making the citation wrong. Checked each of the six by grep to confirm the
quote marks are actually there rather than assuming it.

D1.1 (length of Ex 1.1-1.8) still deliberately not fixed; reasoning unchanged from round 2,
and the validator agrees it is a judgment call for the user rather than a defect.

Verified before submitting: 14 pages, 0 overfull/underfull, no LaTeX warnings, lint clean on
